1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,161
The order book showed only 20 contracts.

2
00:00:08,611 --> 00:00:15,455
Yet 640 contracts traded at that price. And price did not go a single tick higher.

3
00:00:15,905 --> 00:00:20,581
Today: hidden orders, and fake orders.

4
00:00:21,581 --> 00:00:26,570
Hello, this is episode eighteen of the SPIDER Order Flow series.

5
00:00:27,020 --> 00:00:30,729
Last time: liquidity pools and stop hunts.

6
00:00:31,180 --> 00:00:36,586
Today: iceberg orders and spoofing. When to trust the order book.

7
00:00:37,587 --> 00:00:42,994
First, one thing. The order book shows what people intend to do.

8
00:00:43,444 --> 00:00:46,919
The tape and the footprint show what actually happened.

9
00:00:47,369 --> 00:00:50,294
The book is intent. The tape is proof.

10
00:00:51,294 --> 00:00:56,623
An iceberg is a big limit order that shows only a small part in the book.

11
00:00:57,073 --> 00:01:01,436
Like an iceberg: a little on top, a lot below.

12
00:01:01,886 --> 00:01:06,562
As soon as the visible part fills, the next part appears by itself.

13
00:01:07,562 --> 00:01:10,148
Why does a big player hide his size?

14
00:01:10,598 --> 00:01:18,487
If everyone sees a 640 seller up there, buyers back off. Or they sell in front of him.

15
00:01:18,937 --> 00:01:23,378
By hiding it, he fills his whole order at a good price.

16
00:01:24,378 --> 00:01:30,490
Our example. Gold futures, only 20 contracts on the ask at 2650.

17
00:01:30,940 --> 00:01:36,425
Buyers lift it, the 20 fill. And another 20 appears again.

18
00:01:36,875 --> 00:01:38,913
This keeps happening.

19
00:01:39,913 --> 00:01:46,104
Thirty-two refills in five minutes. Thirty-two times twenty: 640 contracts.

20
00:01:46,553 --> 00:01:50,420
And price never touched 2651 once.

21
00:01:50,871 --> 00:01:55,782
The buyers gave up. After that, price fell to 2642.

22
00:01:56,782 --> 00:02:02,502
Three signs. One: repeated refills at the same price.

23
00:02:02,952 --> 00:02:08,203
Two: traded volume at that price is many times the visible size.

24
00:02:08,652 --> 00:02:15,966
Three: price does not move through. Remember absorption from episode sixteen? This can be the reason.

25
00:02:16,966 --> 00:02:22,923
Now the reverse game: spoofing. Show a big order, then pull it before it fills.

26
00:02:23,373 --> 00:02:29,094
The aim: scare or tempt others so that price moves your way.

27
00:02:29,543 --> 00:02:36,075
This is illegal in US futures and in many other places. We are only learning to recognise it.

28
00:02:37,075 --> 00:02:43,501
Suddenly a 900-contract bid appears at 2645. The other rows show 40 to 80.

29
00:02:43,951 --> 00:02:48,862
People think: strong support below. They start buying.

30
00:02:49,311 --> 00:02:57,906
Price reached 2646 and the 900 vanished. Not one contract filled. Then price went to 2638.

31
00:02:58,906 --> 00:03:04,470
So when to trust the book? What filled is more reliable than what was shown.

32
00:03:04,920 --> 00:03:09,831
Do not trust a big order that disappears as price gets close.

33
00:03:10,281 --> 00:03:20,965
And never trade an iceberg on a guess. Wait for price to turn instead of breaking, and for a close, then think. Stop beyond the iceberg.

34
00:03:21,965 --> 00:03:29,123
Spot forex has no single central order book. Your broker's DOM is only its own liquidity.

35
00:03:29,573 --> 00:03:34,902
A real book and tape come from futures. For gold, COMEX gold futures.

36
00:03:35,902 --> 00:03:41,048
So, today in short. The book is intent, the tape is proof.

37
00:03:41,498 --> 00:03:51,372
Iceberg: looks small, fills a lot, price stalls. Spoofing: looks big, vanishes before it fills.

38
00:03:51,822 --> 00:03:56,185
Trust filled volume, not displayed orders.

39
